Q: Understanding execution order and backtracking in recursive subset generation A: <blockquote>
<p>However, my confusion is about what happens <em>after</em> the base case returns. For example, when <code>&quot;abc&quot;</code> is printed, I don’t understand how <code>&quot;ab&quot;</code> is printed next. I am confused about how control returns to the previous recursive call, and why the value of <code>ans</code> becomes <code>&quot;ab&quot;</code> again instead of remaining <code>&quot;abc&quot;</code>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Strings in java are immutable and the value of a string cannot be changed. However, a variable (or expression) can point to a <strong>different</strong> string object, which can have a different value (or the same value). So when you have the expression</p>
<pre><code>ans + ch
</code></pre>
<p>you end up with a <strong>different</strong> string instance. In your case you have the calculated value <code>&quot;abc&quot;</code> from the expression <code>&quot;ab&quot; + 'c'</code> and the recursive method is called with <strong>that</strong> value.</p>
<p>Second, each method call comes with their own &quot;stack frame&quot; of local variables. In combination with that strings are immutable, one &quot;stack frame&quot; could have the local variables (and in your method, these local variables aren't changed):</p>
<pre><code>ans = &quot;ab&quot;
s = &quot;abc&quot;
idx = 1
</code></pre>
<p>and a different method call could have these local variables:</p>
<pre><code>ans = &quot;abc&quot;
s = &quot;abc&quot;
idx = 2
</code></pre>
<p>So when you &quot;go back&quot; from a recursive method call, you &quot;see&quot; that the value of the variable <code>ans</code> is changing from <code>&quot;abc&quot;</code> to <code>&quot;ab&quot;</code>. But in reality, it was just a different local variable from a different &quot;stack frame&quot;.</p>
<p>See the following code executions of <code>subset(&quot;&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 0);</code>:</p>
<pre><code>subset(&quot;&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 0)
- abs = &quot;&quot;
- s = &quot;abc&quot;
- idx = 0
- ch = 'a'

---&gt; subset(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 1)
     - abs = &quot;a&quot;
     - s = &quot;abc&quot;
     - idx = 1
     - ch = 'b'

     ---&gt; subset(&quot;ab&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 2)
        - abs = &quot;ab&quot;
        - s = &quot;abc&quot;
        - idx = 2
        - ch = 'c'

        ---&gt; subset(&quot;abc&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 3)
            - abs = &quot;abc&quot;
            - s = &quot;abc&quot;
            - idx = 3
            (BASE REACHED, PRINT &quot;abc&quot;)

        ---&gt; subset(&quot;ab&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 3)
            - abs = &quot;ab&quot;
            - s = &quot;abc&quot;
            - idx = 3
            (BASE REACHED, PRINT &quot;ab&quot;)


     ---&gt; subset(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 2)
        - abs = &quot;a&quot;
        - s = &quot;abc&quot;
        - idx = 2
        - ch = 'c'

        ---&gt; subset(&quot;ac&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 3)
            - abs = &quot;ac&quot;
            - s = &quot;abc&quot;
            - idx = 3
            (BASE REACHED, PRINT &quot;ac&quot;)

        ---&gt; subset(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 3)
            - abs = &quot;a&quot;
            - s = &quot;abc&quot;
            - idx = 3
            (BASE REACHED, PRINT &quot;a&quot;)

---&gt; subset(&quot;&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 1)
     - abs = &quot;&quot;
     - s = &quot;abc&quot;
     - idx = 1
     - ch = 'b'

     ---&gt; subset(&quot;b&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 2)
        - abs = &quot;b&quot;
        - s = &quot;abc&quot;
        - idx = 2
        - ch = 'c'

        ---&gt; subset(&quot;bc&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 3)
            - abs = &quot;bc&quot;
            - s = &quot;abc&quot;
            - idx = 3
            (BASE REACHED, PRINT &quot;bc&quot;)

        ---&gt; subset(&quot;b&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 3)
            - abs = &quot;b&quot;
            - s = &quot;abc&quot;
            - idx = 3
            (BASE REACHED, PRINT &quot;b&quot;)

     ---&gt; subset(&quot;&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 2)
        - abs = &quot;&quot;
        - s = &quot;abc&quot;
        - idx = 2
        - ch = 'c'

        ---&gt; subset(&quot;c&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 3)
            - abs = &quot;c&quot;
            - s = &quot;abc&quot;
            - idx = 3
            (BASE REACHED, PRINT &quot;c&quot;)

        ---&gt; subset(&quot;&quot;, &quot;abc&quot;, 3)
            - abs = &quot;&quot;
            - s = &quot;abc&quot;
            - idx = 3
            (BASE REACHED, PRINT &quot;&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>This will generate the following output:</p>
<pre><code>abc
ab
ac
a
bc
b
c
       &lt;-- an empty line, since an empty set is a valid subset.
</code></pre>
<p>Notice that each &quot;stack frame&quot; has a different list of variables and in this case, you have/had 15 different &quot;stack frames&quot;. When you &quot;jump back and forth&quot; between stack frames by calling methods or returning from methods, you end up with a new or old stack frame, seeing local variables from <strong>that</strong> stack frame. As mentioned above, the variable <code>abc</code> isn't changed, it is just a different one.</p>
<p>At the end of the execution, all the &quot;stack frames&quot; are gone.</p>

Q: Need PHP code to Oauth2 authenticate and send email via Gmail API without using google-api-php-client A: <p>You missed <a href="https://github.com/googleapis/google-api-php-client?tab=readme-ov-file#cleaning-up-unused-services" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this</a> in the documentation:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Cleaning up unused services</strong></p>
<p>There are over 200 Google API services. The chances are good that you will not want them all. In order to avoid shipping these dependencies with your code, you can run the Google\Task\Composer::cleanup task and specify the services you want to keep in composer.json</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Since you only need the Gmail service, you can add this to your composer.json:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
    &quot;require&quot;: {
        &quot;google/apiclient&quot;: &quot;^2.19&quot;
    },
    &quot;scripts&quot;: {
        &quot;pre-autoload-dump&quot;: &quot;Google\\Task\\Composer::cleanup&quot;
    },
    &quot;extra&quot;: {
        &quot;google/apiclient-services&quot;: [
            &quot;Gmail&quot;
        ]
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And you end up with 818 KB in your vendor/google directory.</p>
